{"id":"3808b288-e228-4f48-a9f1-b02e4b02d4c1","object":"chat.completion","created":1785004622,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be dict\\\")\\n    for key in ['round_scores', 'round_targets', 'forced']:\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"missing {key}\\\")\\n    scores = case['round_scores']\\n    targets = case['round_targets']\\n    forced = case['forced']\\n    if not isinstance(scores, list) or not isinstance(targets, list) or not isinstance(forced, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"invalid types\\\")\\n    if not scores or not targets:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"empty lists\\\")\\n    n_rounds = len(scores)\\n    if len(targets) != n_rounds:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"targets length mismatch\\\")\\n    N = len(scores[0])\\n    for s in scores:\\n        if len(s) != N:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"inconsistent score lengths\\\")\\n    forced_set = set(forced)\\n    if not all(isinstance(idx, int) for idx in forced_set):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"forced indices must be ints\\\")\\n    if any(idx < 0 or idx >= N for idx in forced_set):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"forced index out of range\\\")\\n    active = set(range(N))\\n    history = []\\n    for i in range(n_rounds):\\n        target = targets[i]\\n        score = scores[i]\\n        if target <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"target must be positive\\\")\\n        if len(active) < target:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"not enough active tokens for target\\\")\\n        if len(forced_set) > target:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"more forced tokens than target\\\")\\n        if not forced_set.issubset(active):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"forced index not active\\\")\\n        selected = set(forced_set)\\n        remaining = sorted(active - forced_set, key=lambda idx: (-score[idx], idx))\\n        needed = target - len(forced_set)\\n        if needed < 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"needed < 0\\\")\\n        selected.update(remaining[:needed])\\n        active = selected\\n        history.append(sorted(selected))\\n    retained_indices = sorted(active)\\n    return {\\\"history\\\": history, \\\"retained_indices\\\": retained_indices}\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes a payload with round_scores (list of lists of floats), round_targets (list of ints, number of tokens to retain per round?), forced (list of ints, indices forced to be retained), and possibly other fields? The fixture shows three cases with round_scores each having two rounds (two sublists). round_targets: [6,4] meaning first round retain 6 tokens, second round retain 4? Forced indices are provided per case. The output must have history (list of lists of ints, each inner list is the mask for that round? Actually schema says history is array of arrays of integers, meaning per-round retained indices? Also retained_indices: final list of indices retained after all rounds.\n\nInterpretation: We have multiple rounds. In each round, we have a list of scores for each token (index). We need to select a specified number of tokens (round_targets[i]) from the currently active set, but must include forced indices? The description: \"Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions.\" So forced indices are must-include in each round? Or only in the first round? In the fixtures, forced is a list of indices. For case 0, forced [5]; case 1 forced [0]; case 2 forced [7]. So forced indices are global forced tokens? The objective: iterative token compression: start with all indices active. For each round, we have scores for all original tokens? But the payload only provides scores for each round as arrays of same length? In the fixtures, round_scores has two sublists each of length 8 (since presumably 8 tokens originally). So each round provides a score for each original token? Possibly the scores are from a model for that round. We need to select the top k (round_target) by score, but forced indices must be included even if low score. Also preserve original order when outputting retained indices per round (history) and final retained_indices.\n\nImportant: forced tokens might be outside the active set? They should be kept active always. In each round, we need to select round_target number of tokens from the active set, but forced tokens are always kept regardless of score. So we select forced tokens first, then fill remaining slots with highest-scoring active tokens (excluding duplicates). Then the result for that round is the set of selected indices. However, we need to preserve original order in the output: when we output history, each inner list should be sorted in original order (ascending index). Also retained_indices is final set after all rounds? But note: after each round, the active set becomes the retained indices from that round? Or we perform multiple rounds sequentially reducing token set? The description: \"Begin with all original token indices active. At each round, rank only currently active indices by descending supplied score. Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions. Preserve original order in every retained mask and reject infeasible targets. Return the per-round masks and the final retained indices.\" That suggests that each round uses the scores provided for that round (the scores are for all original indices? But then \"rank only currently active indices\" means we only consider the indices that are still active. But the scores are given per round for all original indices; we can still pick among active ones. However, the forced indices are always active? Or only inserted at each round? Probably forced indices are forced to be included in every round? The description: \"Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions.\" It doesn't say they are only inserted once; they are forced for all rounds. So each round must include forced tokens if they are within the active set? But forced tokens are supposed to be always retained. So they should be part of active set from start and never removed. So in each round, we must include all forced indices (which are always active) and then fill remaining with top-scoring active indices based on that round's scores.\n\nBut careful: round_targets indicates number of tokens to keep after that round? For first round target 6, second target 4. That means after first round we keep 6 tokens; after second we keep 4. So we reduce from 8 to 6 to 4. Forced indices are among those 6 and then among those 4? If forced index is 5, it should be in final 4? Yes.\n\nThe algorithm: start with active set = all indices 0..N-1. For each round i: given scores list scores[i] (length N), round_target = round_targets[i]. We need to select exactly round_target indices from active set such that forced indices subset is included. The selection is based on scores: among active indices, we assign priority: forced indices get highest priority (they must be selected). Then we sort remaining active indices by descending score, and take top (round_target - len(forced))? But careful: forced indices might already be in the active set; we force them to be selected. However, there might be more forced indices than round_target? That would be infeasible -> raise ValueError. Also if len(active) < round_target? Infeasible.\n\nSo for each round:\n- active_set = set of currently retained indices (starting with all).\n- forced_set = set(forced) (should be subset of active_set? If not, raise)\n- If len(active_set) < round_target: infeasible.\n- If len(forced_set) > round_target: infeasible.\n- Then choose: include forced_set automatically.\n- From remaining active indices (active_set - forced_set), pick the top (round_target - len(forced_set)) by descending score in that round's scores list. If tie, presumably stable by original index? The spec says \"All ranking and traversal ties use the source position or identifier specified above; never depend on hash-map iteration.\" For ranking, tie-breaking by index (lower index should be considered higher? Actually descending score, but if scores equal, we need deterministic order. Typically we should use index as tiebreaker ascending? But spec says \"source position or identifier\", so we can sort by (score, index) with descending score and then ascending index for tie. Or maybe keep original order after ranking? But we are selecting top k, so we need deterministic. I'll use descending score, then ascending index.\n- Then the new active_set is the union of forced_set and the selected top candidates. But note: forced_set might already be included; we just need to build the list of selected indices. Then the retained indices for that round (history) should be sorted in ascending order (preserve original order). The final retained_indices after all rounds is the active_set after last round, also sorted.\n\nReturn: {\"history\": [list of list of ints for each round], \"retained_indices\": list of ints}.\n\nEdge cases: forced might include indices that are not in initial set? That's invalid. Also round_targets length must equal number of score lists. In fixtures, both are 2. So that's fine.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Must be pure Python.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Validate case: must have 'round_scores', 'round_targets', 'forced'. Each is list. round_scores must be list of lists of floats (or numbers). round_targets list of ints. forced list of ints.\n- N = len(round_scores[0]) (assume all same length). Check that all score lists have same length N. Also round_targets length equals number of score lists.\n- active = set(range(N))\n- forced_set = set(forced)\n- If forced_set - active: raise ValueError\n- history = []\n- For each round i:\n    - target = round_targets[i]\n    - scores = round_scores[i]\n    - if len(active) < target: raise\n    - if len(forced_set) > target: raise (since forced must be included)\n    - remaining_active = sorted(active - forced_set, key=lambda idx: (-scores[idx], idx))  # descending score, ascending idx for tie\n    - needed = target - len(forced_set)\n    - if needed < 0: raise (already caught)\n    - selected = list(forced_set) + remaining_active[:needed]\n    - # Now the new active set is these selected indices\n    - active = set(selected)\n    - # history: sort selected in ascending order\n    - history.append(sorted(selected))\n- retained_indices = sorted(active)\n- return {\"history\": history, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}\n\nBut careful: forced_set is the same across rounds? Yes, forced indices are always forced to be included. So we must ensure they are in active after each round. In the selection, we include forced_set each round. That's correct.\n\nBut what about the case where forced index is not in active because it was removed earlier? That can't happen because we always include forced_set in selection. So they persist.\n\nNow test with fixtures:\nCase 0: round_scores two lists of 8. round_targets [6,4], forced [5].\nRound1: active set = {0..7}. forced_set={5}. target=6. needed=5. Sort remaining 7 indices by score descending. Scores first round: indices:0:0.447995,1:0.883732,2:0.480372,3:0.310493,4:0.996002,5:0.516433 (but 5 forced),6:0.687522,7:0.339436. So sorted: idx4(0.996), idx1(0.8837), idx6(0.6875), idx2(0.4803), idx0(0.4479), idx5(0.5164? Actually 5 is forced, not in remaining), idx3(0.3105), idx7(0.3394). Wait 5 is not in remaining. So top5: 4,1,6,2,0. So selected = {5} + {4,1,6,2,0} = {0,1,2,4,5,6}. Sorted: [0,1,2,4,5,6]. That's 6 indices.\nRound2: active set = {0,1,2,4,5,6}. forced_set={5}. target=4. needed=3. Scores second round: 0:0.070941,1:0.346020,2:0.538260,4:0.398656,5:0.708900,6:0.770823. (note no index3,7). Sort remaining active except forced: indices 0,1,2,4,6. Scores: 6(0.7708), 2(0.53826), 5 forced, 4(0.39866), 1(0.34602), 0(0.07094). Top3: 6,2,4. So selected = {5} + {6,2,4} = {2,4,5,6}. Sorted: [2,4,5,6]. So retained_indices = [2,4,5,6]. Check if any other logic? The history: [[0,1,2,4,5,6], [2,4,5,6]]? But note: round1 history is the selected after round1. That matches.\n\nBut is forced always included? Yes. The spec says \"Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions.\" That matches.\n\nNow case1: forced [0]. Round1 target6: forced idx0. remaining top5: scores round1: indices 0 forced, 1:0.2538,2:0.36588,3:0.3409,4:0.6303,5:0.9195,6:0.01083,7:0.9582. Sort remaining except 0: 7(0.9582),5(0.9195),4(0.6303),2(0.3659),3(0.3409),1(0.2538),6(0.0108). Top5: 7,5,4,2,3. So selected = {0,7,5,4,2,3} -> sorted [0,2,3,4,5,7]. Round2 target4: active {0,2,3,4,5,7}, forced 0. needed 3. Scores round2: 0:0.79689,2:0.60183,3:0.55234,4:0.41927,5:0.8915,7:0.08045. Sort remaining: 5(0.8915),0 forced, 2(0.6018),3(0.5523),4(0.4193),7(0.0805). Top3: 5,2,3. Selected = {0,5,2,3} -> sorted [0,2,3,5]. So retained_indices [0,2,3,5]. case2 forced7: similarly compute. Likely similar.\n\nThus implementation is straightforward.\n\nNow, about \"reject infeasible targets\": if round_target > len(active) or forced > target, raise ValueError. Also if forced indices not in initial set, raise.\n\nWe also need to ensure that round_targets list length equals number of rounds (len(round_scores)). Also round_scores must be non-empty and each sublist length > 0.\n\nNow output must be JSON exactly as per schema: \"history\" array of arrays of integers, \"retained_indices\" array of integers. No extra fields.\n\nNow produce the implementation string. We'll wrap in JSON with key \"implementation\". Note: need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Write Python source as a string.\n\nI'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validation\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be dict\")\n    for key in ['round_scores', 'round_targets', 'forced']:\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing {key}\")\n    scores = case['round_scores']\n    targets = case['round_targets']\n    forced = case['forced']\n    if not isinstance(scores, list) or not isinstance(targets, list) or not isinstance(forced, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid types\")\n    if not scores or not targets:\n        raise ValueError(\"empty lists\")\n    n_rounds = len(scores)\n    if len(targets) != n_rounds:\n        raise ValueError(\"targets length mismatch\")\n    N = len(scores[0])\n    for s in scores:\n        if len(s) != N:\n            raise ValueError(\"inconsistent score lengths\")\n    # Check forced indices are valid\n    forced_set = set(forced)\n    if not all(isinstance(idx, int) for idx in forced_set):\n        raise ValueError(\"forced indices must be ints\")\n    if any(idx < 0 or idx >= N for idx in forced_set):\n        raise ValueError(\"forced index out of range\")\n    active = set(range(N))\n    history = []\n    for i in range(n_rounds):\n        target = targets[i]\n        score = scores[i]\n        if target <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"target must be positive\")\n        if len(active) < target:\n            raise ValueError(\"not enough active tokens for target\")\n        if len(forced_set) > target:\n            raise ValueError(\"more forced tokens than target\")\n        # forced indices must be active\n        if not forced_set.issubset(active):\n            raise ValueError(\"forced index not active\")\n        # select forced first\n        selected = set(forced_set)\n        # remaining active indices excluding forced\n        remaining = sorted(active - forced_set, key=lambda idx: (-score[idx], idx))\n        needed = target - len(forced_set)\n        if needed < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"needed < 0\")\n        selected.update(remaining[:needed])\n        # update active set to selected\n        active = selected\n        history.append(sorted(selected))\n    retained_indices = sorted(active)\n    return {\"history\": history, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}\n\nBut careful: forced_set is global same across rounds. Works.\n\nNote: The spec says \"Do not mutate case\". We don't.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON with escaped string. Use Python to generate the JSON string. Since we are typing the answer manually, we must escape. I'll write the implementation in a string and then use json.dumps in mind. But in the answer, I need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the escaped Python source. So I'll construct the source, then escape.\n\nI'll double-check for any missing details: The function must be named solve and take case argument. Return dict. Use only standard library. No random.\n\nThe fixture shows \"_case_nonce\" field but we ignore it.\n\nAlso need to handle potential ties correctly: sorting by (-score, idx) ensures stable. Good.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1483,"completion_tokens":4205,"total_tokens":5688,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3641},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":75},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}